      "content": "In sourdough, lactobacilli and yeast have a synergistic relationship. The lactobacilli ferment the carbohydrates in the flour, producing lactic and acetic acid, which adds sourness to the bread. Meanwhile, wild yeasts or cultivated baker's yeast consume these acids and produce carbon dioxide gas and ethanol, which help the dough rise and contribute to the bread's flavor. This balanced interaction results in the signature tangy taste and chewy crumb of sourdough bread.",
